From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-controller: a policy seen for the first time is a change

cntlr_check_ttlm_policy_change() deliberately does not treat the first sighting
of /etc/mapc/ttlm-policy after a start as a change. The reasoning - "the agents
were configured from this same file the last time round, and pushing on every
restart would put a CMDU on the air for nothing" - does not hold.

A negotiated TTLM belongs to an association, not to a box. When a backhaul bSTA
re-associates its map is gone, and nothing re-applies it:
agent_process_tid_to_link_policy() is reached only from an incoming Service
Prioritization Request (agent_map.c:7350). Measured 2026-09-22: all four
backhaul bSTAs re-associated within 16 s of each other at 06:54 (`associated
at` 1790052884177 / 891116 / 896175 / 900291 ms), which is not a rare event -
it is a normal morning in this lab.

So after a controller restart the mesh runs with no TID policy, the file on
disk says it has one, and nothing anywhere says otherwise. There is also no
second chance: the policy is sent with send_cmdu() and no cookie
(cntlr_cmdu.c:975), so it is one unicast with no retry and no ack tracking.

Cost of the change: one unicast CMDU per agent NAMED IN THE FILE, once, at
start. Agents the file does not name cost one cheap read and send nothing
(cntlr_gen_tid_to_link_policy returns 0). A controller with no policy file at
all is untouched - the stat() fails and this never runs.

Hit again on 2026-09-23: a policy file written after the controller had
started sent nothing until it was rewritten; the rewrite then reached the
radios of both ends in 2 s.


diff -urN a/src/cntlr.c b/src/cntlr.c
--- a/src/cntlr.c	2026-09-22 08:01:21.912827496 +0200
+++ b/src/cntlr.c	2026-09-22 08:01:23.291467786 +0200
@@ -1963,6 +1963,19 @@
  * Deliberately mtime and not a hash: the producer is mlo-steerd rewriting the
  * whole file, and a rewrite always moves mtime. Comparing content would cost a
  * read on every tick to catch a case that cannot happen.
+ *
+ * 2026-09-22: the first sighting after a start IS a change, and used not to be.
+ * The reasoning for skipping it - "the agents were configured from this same
+ * file the last time round" - does not hold. A negotiated TTLM belongs to an
+ * association, not to a box: when a backhaul bSTA re-associates, its map is
+ * gone, and nothing re-applies it (agent_process_tid_to_link_policy is reached
+ * only from an incoming Service Prioritization Request). On 2026-09-22 at 06:54
+ * all four backhaul bSTAs re-associated within 16 s of each other, so the
+ * window in which "they still have it" is true is hours at best.
+ *
+ * The cost of being wrong the other way is one unicast CMDU per named agent,
+ * once, at controller start. The cost of today's behaviour is a mesh that
+ * silently runs with no TID policy after every restart and says nothing.
  */
 static bool cntlr_check_ttlm_policy_change(struct controller *c)
 {
@@ -1981,15 +1994,14 @@
 	if (st.st_mtime == last_mtime)
 		return false;
 
-	/* First sighting after a start is not a change - the agents were
-	 * configured from this same file the last time round, and pushing on
-	 * every restart would put a CMDU on the air for nothing. */
-	if (last_mtime) {
+	if (last_mtime)
 		info("%s: ttlm-policy changed, sending Service Prioritization Request\n",
-		    __func__);
-		changed = true;
-	}
+		     __func__);
+	else
+		info("%s: ttlm-policy seen for the first time, sending Service Prioritization Request\n",
+		     __func__);
 
+	changed = true;
 	last_mtime = st.st_mtime;
 
 	return changed;
